hat you would see when you  enter the reception area of an overseas  cemetery a photograph of the current  commander-in-chief when this picture was  taken it was President Obama then you  have the Secretary of the Commission and  the head of the European office it was  Max Cleland and Mike do you recognized  that picture on the right okay general  general Hawkins and then underneath you  have the the whole collection of former  chairman starting with Pershing to the  left and general Frederick Franks who  was a George W Bush appointee who was  removed from the position on the day  that President Obama took office but  general Merrill McDermott choice for  chairman had not been in office long  enough to be pictured there but this is  a statue of General Pershing in his  hometown in Missouri and he  any of you know the hometown I don't  even think there's a traffic light there  it's in the northwestern part of  Missouri it's place called Laclede  Pershing left there in 1881 I believe to  go to West Point and the rest as they  say is history now this is the arguably  the most important single World War one  monument in France it's on a hill called  mole coal in the meuse-argonne of battle  area it's a hundred seventy five foot  high Doric column capped by a Statue of  Liberty and the Statue of Liberty on top  is looking out into the infinity of time  and space in the direction from which  the American troops came the battle the  meuse-argonne began on the 26th of  September 1918 it ended on Armistice Day  at 11 o'clock in the morning 47 day long  battle it's the bloodiest battle in  American history 122 thousand casualties  dead and wounded together  it's the bloodiest battle in American  history because it was a very intense  battle but it also lasted over a long  period of time the Battle of the Bulge  is a very close second in terms of the  overall casualties from 1944 45 but this  monument was dedicated on the 1st of  August 1937 General Pershing was there  marshal petah was there the commander of  the French Army during the last couple  years of the First World War the  president of the French Republic was  there and President Roosevelt was on his  yacht in the Potomac but he gave a live  radio address that the audience heard  most of the Frenchmen of course couldn't  understand it but it was something of a  path-breaking undertaking and it came  off successfully for a live radio  broadcast to actually reach its audience  the monument was erected  on the ruins of a village called Mo  Falcone and that's the ruins of the  village Church the French government  gave us the ground but we didn't really  take full possession of it until we'd  worked out a liability agreement with  the French because they considered it a  dangerous place and it probably still is  the ground underneath its honeycombed  with underground bunkers there still if  you look hard enough you can cut  yourself on rusty barbed wire even to  this day the architecture of the  monument was John Russell Pope who later  did the Jefferson Memorial and had a  very testy relationship Polk did with  the Secretary of the Commission at the  time but he was given this this chore in  recognition of the fact that he was one  of the great architects of his day the  next three shots are just shots from the  top of the memorial and as you look at  each one realize again that you're  looking at the bloodiest battlefield in  American history the monument occupies a  commanding spot you can one can see  miles from there and one can see the  monument miles away from it and all  that's deliberate Pershing one of these  memorials to be seen from miles away  because he hoped that anyone who saw the  monument would just have to come get a  closer look and actually visit it and  anyone who visited there would be  reminded of what the Americans had  contributed to the war effort  the second biggest monument of World War  1 VIN each is on a hill called mult SEC  south of Verdun  it's was in the Santee Hell sector the  Battle of San Miguel began on the 12th  of September 1918 it was the first major  battle that the United States fought as  an independent army and this monument  crowns its height  for this day first you saw perspective  from distance now you see it closer up  the next you're gonna see the view from  the memorial  so this monument was actually rather  heavily damaged during World War two by  American tank fire the Germans were  there in the closing weeks of August or  beginning weeks of September the  Americans had to blast them out of there  and the monument was hit several times  but easily enough repaired in the  aftermath of of the war now this  monument is is the third most important  monument it's on on Hill 204 which is  military language for hill outside  chateau-thierry in France about 35 miles  east of Paris the architect for the  monument was the first consulting  architect for the Commission a Frenchman  transplanted to Philadelphia where he  was on the faculty of the school of  architecture at the University of  Pennsylvania  Paul Philippe Cret was his name C ret he  designed that memorial it's DN Marne  memorial the Commission just opened up a  new visitor center underneath the  memorial which I was able to visit last  spring it's it's an extraordinarily fine  a piece of work very informative panels  and even they even have some footage of  Pershing giving his dedicatory address  at the meuse-argonne monument and here's  the ever-present map to which I made  reference earlier it's designed to  acquaint people who visit the monument  with some of the action that took place  around it one of the smaller memorials  World War one memorials there were 11  World War 1 memorials altogether is in  the village of Cantigny some of you may  know that the first division saw action  there at the end of May 1918 they drove  the Germans out of this village and then  man  just hold it against the inevitable  German counter-attack and it was a big  plus for the Americans the first time  really that we'd been bloodied in an  action that we took pretty much by  ourselves and the monument is right in  the center of the little village in peak  rd i think it is in france and then the  ever-present map and explanatory panels  again this monument is on top of a ridge  in sham Ponyo it's called near the  village of Psalm P the dedicatory  plaqued they're saying that the monument  is plainly erected to remind people how  grateful the United States is for the  service of the men in this area the  soldiers in this area this is the view  from the top of the memorial you can see  down in the foreground there German  trenches that still survived they're  actually meant to survive and this just  simply re-emphasize the point that the  agency the ABMC was very much intent to  put its cemeteries and its monuments  right on the battlefields there are our  individual panels along the base of the  memorial honoring the divisions that did  the bulk of the fighting there you see  the second 36 division and this other  plaque refers to the 42nd division that  was the rainbow division so many of the  divisions in world war 1 or organized  state by state but the 42nd division was  known as the rainbow division that's the  division Douglas MacArthur fought with  and that's because it literally had  soldiers in it from coast to coast like  a rainbow stretching across the  continent this is what the cemeteries  actually look like a very different look  from what you see in Arlington for one  thing the headstones are much higher off  the ground and they are overtly  I dare say unapologetically on a bad  should ly religious shapes Latin cross  for the Christian dead star of david'  for the Jewish dead this is from the  meuse-argonne cemetery which is the  largest  abmc cemetery in Europe there are over  14,000 men buried there and about half  the total dead from that battle and very  sad to say that no American president  has ever visited there that's perhaps  all the evidence we need to suggest how  little remembered even in the centennial  year I fear to say the Great War is for  us every cemetery has a chapel  this is the meuse-argonne chapel in  Romanesque style chapel is on the  highest part of the cemetery so there's  a very panoramic view approaching it  this palm was actually left on the altar  of the chapel on dedication day for the  cemetery which was Memorial Day 1937 it  was left there by the President of  France and he was there in general  Pershing's company this is what the  cemeteries look like on Memorial Day  each individual grave is decorated in  this case because this is a French  cemetery this is the Oise in cemetery  our second largest World War 1 Cemetery  you see an American flag and a flag of  the host country if it were Belgium it  would be a Belgian flag in Italy an  Italian flag from World War two so forth  and so on so but it makes for a  spectacular sight and all those flags  are put in by hand as well this is what  the interior of a chapel can look like  this Isabella wood chapel but again  unabashedly Christian symbols inside  inscriptions stained-glass windows and  this one I can't see them but they're  there  and up and down the walls in this  particular case names of the missing as  well each of the cemeteries obviously  honors those buried there but there are  walls of the missing each place so the  total number of soldiers honored is  actually in excess of those with  individual interments at the end of  World War one families were given the  opportunity to create a verse or some  memorable passage that they would like  to have engraved on the back of the  headstone of their dead loved one this  is an idea that we took from the British  the British created their war cemeteries  before we did and for those of you who  ever visited either a World War one or  World War two cemetery maintained by the  Commonwealth War Graves Commission of  the British government you'll see just  about every grave at the base of it has  a very moving inscription first time I  ever visited such a cemetery was in  bayou and Normandy and my father was  along as the World War two veteran and  he read about six or eight of the  inscriptions and and just couldn't go  any further they're very personal very  loving very moving but for some reason  the people of the United States didn't  rise to that challenge they're only 19  headstones in the 29,000 plus headstones  with names on them that have those  inscriptions the crosses are of Carrara  marble Italian marble with a beautiful  silvery vein to it it was quite a  controversy about that the idea that we  were going to get our material for the  headstones from Italy as opposed to  using good old American granite the  American granite Association pitched  quite a fit at a succession of  Commission meetings finally General  Pershing had to tell him in 1926 that  the issue was closed and they weren't to  come back and try to browbeat the  Commission anymore  or in fact the Italian marble came in at  low bid granite would have been three or  four times more expensive per headstone  but it was the the purity of the  whiteness that ultimately I think  clinched the deal this is the the  gravestone of the son of one of the  original members of the Commission a  woman by the name of Jacqueline Cody  Bentley she was a gold star mother from  Chicago she was one of the original  seven commissioners her son had died in  1917 he's buried in the Oise aend  cemetery and I have a picture a couple  pictures in the in the book of mrs.  Bentley and also General Pershing at  that grave when the entire Commission  went over there in 1924 to inspect the  cemeteries and the monument sites this  is the only piece of privately donated  artwork in any of the american cemetery  is a beautiful statue of an american  Doughboy given by a mother whose son is  buried in the Santee Hill Cemetery  that's where the statue is she paid  $30,000 for this the end of the 1920s  and the Commission initially voted not  to allow the statue in there because  they didn't want to have to rule on  individual submissions for private  donations but when General Pershing  found out a couple years later that the  woman in question was the daughter of  James G Blaine who was Republican  nominee for president in 1884 he changed  his mind and Pershing as a young man  greatly admired mr. Blaine so there the  statue is but if they were going to make  an exception over anything I'm glad they  did it with that one  this is Superintendent Michael Koontz  some years ago Michael has posted at the  Normandy cemetery now I believe he's  director of visitor services over there  but in 2010 when that picture was taken  he was superintendent it's at me he'll  and he's taken the flags down which was  done every day at every Cemetery to  symbolize a duty day  completed and I've heard once cemetery  superintendent say this I've read it  elsewhere that there's a belief and I  think for very very sound and poignant  reasons is every soldier buried in one  of those cemeteries resting abroad is  still serving his country by reminding  us all of the price of freedom this is  the Cambridge Cemetery in England the  world war ii cemetery now you can see a  little bit difference in architectural  style there are more rectangular chapel  building for example we don't argue  about whether a chapel is in the case of  the World War one chapel is gothic or  Romanesque with these they're more  modern they're about 3600 of our  soldiers buried in Cambridge  the majority of them Flyers and here's  the wall of the missing at Cambridge  Cemetery and there are actually more  names on the wall the missing then there  are buried in the cemetery there were  5000 names on the walls of missing two  of them would be very well known they  suspect everyone here Glenn Miller and  Joseph Kennedy jr. so many were lost of  course in bombing raids and whatnot lost  in places where their bodies just simply  couldn't be recovered the ever-present  battle maps this is Cambridge again  every cemetery chapel has a memorial an  article of remembrance to commemorate  the Jewish dead this is ten Roman  numerals signifying the ten commandments  with the Star of David and there you see  a more of a perspective but the Jewish  symbol is still there at the base of  that right hand pillar this is the  Brittany Cemetery built very much in the  style of the local parish church with  the chapel in the middle built of stone  that is native to the area  beautiful flora very colorful cemetery  it's it's actually the second cemetery  that we have in Normandy but I'm just  taking a wild guess but I'll bet for  every one person who visits Brittany ten  or twenty go to the one that overlooks  Omaha Beach unabashedly religious  inscriptions in the interior of the  chapel designed to comfort those who  visit and then finally we come to what  has to be the best known of all the ABMC  sites the norm in the American Cemetery  this was Memorial Day 2011 Old Glory  flies next to the French tricolour on  Memorial Day each of the graves  decorated with flags of the respective  countries off in the distance this  picture you can see the the ocean the  channel when our soldiers landed on  d-day once they fought their way off the  beach and up the bluffs they came right  across the ground where the cemetery  currently is located currently it's not  going anywhere  I promise you Normandy again  again  next picture is of two of the sons of  President Theodore Roosevelt on the  right hand side the grave the headstone  inscription is accentuated because  Theodore Roosevelt jr. was a Medal of  Honor winner but next to him is his  younger brother Quentin who actually  died in the First World War but at the  request of the Roosevelt family after  the Second World War when the oldest of  the Roosevelt brothers wound up at  Normandy Quentin's grave was moved  it's this pair of graves I think there  are over 40 sets of brothers in the  normandy cemetery to this day but this  is one of the most frequently visited  spots this is the new visitor center  dedicated in 2007 and Mike just told me  over dinner tonight it's being renovated  again hopefully ready in time for the  75th anniversary next year where when  there will surely be a new crush of  visitors including the heads of state  who I'm sure will be there on June 6th  and finally now this picture the  white-haired lady is the world war two  Widow Billy Harris sister Jude me Peggy  Harris is her name her husband Billy is  buried in the cemetery he was shot down  in June or July of 1944 he was 22 years  old they had just married prior to his  going off into the service for about 60  years  Peggy was not aware that her husband was  actually in the cemetery but she  ultimately learned that that's where he  was so she has made a visit I don't know  that she's able to make the trip anymore  but for a string of years annually she  would be over there on Memorial Day she  never remarried because she never really  knew for certain until she learned that  her husband was buried in Normandy that  he was in fact dead so everybody knows  her that's her receiving a red rose from  a member of  the cemetery staff and that was a  Memorial Day 2011 as well and I'd like  to end with this because her story is  just one of thousands and thousands of  stories because every one of the  headstones marks the grave of someone  who was connected to in life to a  network of friends and family and the  cemeteries are meant of course to honor  and remember all of those those dead  soldiers but it's a call upon us to do  the same so thank you very much  [Applause]
 

